  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n    Central idea: Optimize H as the optimal solution for a continuous relaxation of the discrete problem, leveraging periodicity. We use a smooth function representation with periodic spline interpolation to obtain a globally smooth function that avoids the boolean jumps of the step function approach. The optimization alternates between two scales: a global scale (long-range positions) and local scales (feature widths) to minimize correlations. This differs from discretization-based techniques by maintaining smoothness throughout optimization.\n\n 2. Mathematical specification\n    - Variables: Coefficients for periodic B-splines converted into a Wannier basis to enforce global behavior across [0,2]\n    - Objective: Minimize C\u2085 using the softmax penalization: exp(-T*C\u2085) across lags incorporated into the energy function\n    - Constraints: Integral = 1 enforced via penalty terms and automatic normalization\n\n 3. Implementation procedure\n    - Section 3.1 Resolution selection\n        Base resolution = 32 (corresponding to 0.03125dx)\n        Upgrading path: [64,128,256,512,1024] via adaptive condition monitoring\n    - Section 3.2 Feasibility check\n        Use normalized asinh(x-c) to penalize boundaries with adaptive clipping thresholds\n    - Section 3.3 Multiscale decomposition\n        Long-range positions controlled by tangent descriptors (derivative constraints)\n        Local features controlled by curvature-like terms in the Lagrangian\n    - Section 3.4 Warm-start management\n        Initial condition: If parent solution exists, transform via signature filtering\n        Progressive sample selection using tangent matching\n\n 4. Search choices\n    - Step 1: Global optimization using derivative-free bound-constrained (BFGS)\n    - Step 2: Post-processing using adjoint sensitivity method\n    - Dynamic sample rate: \u219380% for global, \u219120% for local optimization\n    - Objective annealing: T starts at 1e3 then follows exponential \u2193 with warm-up period\n\n 5. Compute budget and robustness\n    - Memory-plan dominant costs:\n        Primary: Grid interpolation (\u223c(n_points) operations)\n        Secondary: Sparse matrix multiplies (O(min(n, n^2)))\n    - Time allocation:\n        25% for global optimization\n        35% for local optimization\n        25% for adaptive resolution\n        15% validation fallback\n    - Handling boundary cases:\n        Checkpoint every \u223c25% of budget with parent retention\n\n 6. Validation and return contract\n    - Verify integral within 1e-6 tolerance\n    - Ensure all values are within [0.88*min_parent, 1.12*max_parent] with clipping applied if necessary\n    - Return {np.asarray(h), c5_bound, n_points} with shape (n_points,)\n    - Reject any normalizations >1e-4 deviation from parent's integral",
